# Rails Conventions

## Project Structure

Follow standard Rails conventions:

```
project_root/
├── app/
│   ├── controllers/
│   ├── models/
│   ├── views/
│   └── jobs/
├── config/
│   ├── database.yml
│   ├── routes.rb
│   └── puma.rb
├── db/
│   ├── migrate/
│   └── schema.rb
├── Gemfile
└── Procfile
```

## Controllers

For API-only apps, inherit from `ActionController::API`:

```ruby
class ItemsController < ApplicationController
  def index
    items = Item.all
    render json: items
  end

  def create
    item = Item.new(item_params)
    if item.save
      render json: item, status: :created
    else
      render json: { errors: item.errors }, status: :unprocessable_entity
    end
  end

  private

  def item_params
    params.require(:item).permit(:name, :description)
  end
end
```

## Models

```ruby
class Item < ApplicationRecord
  validates :name, presence: true

  scope :recent, -> { order(created_at: :desc) }
end
```

Always create migrations for schema changes:

```bash
rails generate migration AddDescriptionToItems description:text
rails db:migrate
```

## Routes

```ruby
Rails.application.routes.draw do
  resources :items, only: [:index, :show, :create, :update, :destroy]

  get "/health", to: proc { [200, {}, ['{"status":"ok"}']] }
end
```

## Database Configuration

Use `DATABASE_URL` for production:

```yaml
production:
  url: <%= ENV["DATABASE_URL"] %>
```

## Health Check

Always include a health endpoint in routes:

```ruby
get "/health", to: proc { [200, { "Content-Type" => "application/json" }, ['{"status":"ok"}']] }
```
